reviews.targetStatement:
Test a simple null $H_0:\theta=\theta_0$ against a simple alternative $H_1:\theta=\theta_1$, where the data $X$ have density (or mass function) $f_0$ or $f_1$. For $k\ge0$ let $R_k=\{x: f_1(x)>k\,f_0(x)\}$ be the **likelihood-ratio region**, and suppose its size is $\alpha=P_0(X\in R_k)$ (randomize on the boundary $\{f_1=kf_0\}$ if needed to reach exactly $\alpha$). Then

(a) **(sufficiency)** $R_k$ is a **most powerful** level-$\alpha$ test: every region $R$ with $P_0(X\in R)\le\alpha$ has $P_1(X\in R)\le P_1(X\in R_k)$;

(b) **(necessity)** every most powerful level-$\alpha$ test agrees with a likelihood-ratio test except on $\{f_1=kf_0\}$ and a null set, and has size exactly $\alpha$ unless it has power 1.

For $\mathbf x,\mathbf y,\mathbf z\in\R^k$ and real $\alpha$:
(a) $|\mathbf x|\ge0$;
(b) $|\mathbf x|=0$ only when $\mathbf x=\mathbf 0$;
(c) $|\alpha\mathbf x|=|\alpha|\,|\mathbf x|$;
(d) $|\mathbf x\cdot\mathbf y|\le|\mathbf x|\,|\mathbf y|$;
(e) $|\mathbf x+\mathbf y|\le|\mathbf x|+|\mathbf y|$;
(f) $|\mathbf x-\mathbf z|\le|\mathbf x-\mathbf y|+|\mathbf y-\mathbf z|$.

The basic convex sets of $\R^n$ are
(a) a **hyperplane** $\{x:a^\top x=b\}$ and the **halfspace** $\{x:a^\top x\le b\}$, $a\ne0$;
(b) a **Euclidean ball** $B(x_c,r)=\{x:\norm{x-x_c}_2\le r\}$ — indeed every norm ball $\{x:\norm{x-x_c}\le r\}$;
(c) an **ellipsoid** $\{x:(x-x_c)^\top P^{-1}(x-x_c)\le1\}$ with $P\in\mathbb S^n_{++}$ (symmetric positive definite), whose semi-axes are $\sqrt{\lambda_i(P)}$ along the eigenvectors of $P$;
(d) a **polyhedron** $\{x:Ax\preceq b,\ Cx=d\}$, a finite intersection of halfspaces and hyperplanes, and a **polytope** if it is bounded.
All of them are convex.

Suppose $T \in \mathcal L(V, W)$. Then
(a) $T$ is injective $\iff$ $0$ is not a singular value of $T$;
(b) the number of positive singular values of $T$ equals $\dim \operatorname{range} T$;
(c) $T$ is surjective $\iff$ the number of positive singular values of $T$ equals $\dim W$.

